{
  "dom_id": "step-q94247",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\u223cN(-2,{3}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<-4.28).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1977\\,$ | $\\,0.1949\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2266\\,$ | $\\,0.2236\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2578\\,$ | $\\,0.2546\\,$ | $\\,0.2514\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z\u223cN(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-2$ and $\\sigma =3.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-4.28) & =P(Z<\\frac{-4.28-(-2)}{3}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.76) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.76) \\\\  & =\u03a6(-0.76)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/56f9956d27f7527106869001273833c863d26053baf8c624c98d4ec2fb3c7f7b.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\u03a6(-0.76)=0.2236.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<-4.28)=0.2236.\n$$",
